{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014P1iA7g6kGcF8AYXZy3PWT","type":"tool_result","content":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Builds the root Dockerfile and checks the image:\n#   - one build for linux/amd64 + linux/arm64 into an OCI layout: the index lists exactly those two platforms\nARG GO_IMAGE=golang:1.27.4-trixie@sha256:f18c3d73a184fb0582828510722e723b63a93f7188d11dd223d9be6f6880e65d\n\nFROM --platform=$BUILDPLATFORM ${GO_IMAGE} AS build\nARG TARGETOS\nARG TARGETARCH\nARG VERSION=dev\nARG GOVULNCHECK_VERSION=v1.10.0\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY go.mod go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download\nCOPY . .\nRUN mkdir -p /out && \\\n    CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=${TARGETOS} GOARCH=${TARGETARCH} \\\nif [ \"$out\" = \"ard $version\" ]; then pass \"ard version prints the stamped version\"\nelse fail \"ard version prints the stamped version\" \"got '$out', want 'ard $version'\"; fi\n\ntools_ok() {\n  run --entrypoint go \"$tag\" version | grep -q '^go version go1\\.27\\.4 ' || { echo \"go: $(run --entrypoint go \"$tag\" version)\" >&2; return 1; }\n  run --entrypoint govulncheck \"$tag\" -version 2>&1 | grep -q 'govulncheck@v1\\.10\\.0' || { echo \"govulncheck: $(run --entrypoint govulncheck \"$tag\" -version 2>&1 | head -3)\" >&2; return 1; }\n  run --entrypoint osv-scanner \"$tag\" --version 2>&1 | grep -q 'osv-scanner version: 2\\.7\\.1' || { echo \"osv-scanner: $(run --entrypoint osv-scanner \"$tag\" --version 2>&1 | head -2)\" >&2; return 1; }\n  [ \"$(run --entrypoint jq \"$tag\" --version)\" = \"jq-1.8.2\" ] || { echo \"jq: $(run --entrypoint jq \"$tag\" --version)\" >&2; return 1; }\n  run --entrypoint git \"$tag\" --version | grep -q '^git version ' || return 1\n  [ \"$(run --entrypoint sh \"$tag\" -c 'command -v ard')\" = \"/usr/local/bin/ard\" ] || return 1\n}\nif tools_ok; then pass \"tools on PATH at pinned versions\"; else fail \"tools on PATH at pinned versions\"; fi\n\nuid=\"$(run --entrypoint id \"$tag\" -u):$(run --entrypoint id \"$tag\" -g)\"\nif [ \"$uid\" = \"65532:65532\" ]; then pass \"default uid is 65532\"; else fail \"default uid is 65532\" \"got $uid\"; fi\n\nlabel() { docker image inspect \"$1\" --format \"{{ index .Config.Labels \\\"$2\\\" }}\"; }\nif [ \"$(label \"$tag\" org.opencontainers.image.revision)\" = \"$revision\" ] && \\\n   [ \"$(label \"$tag\" org.opencontainers.image.source)\" = \"https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard\" ]; then\n  pass \"revision and source labels are set\"\nelse fail \"revision and source labels are set\"; fi\n\n# The cve role as ard serve runs it in a container: a host subprocess in this image, cwd a read-only worktree, HOME writable.\nscan_out=\"$work/scan.out\"\nif run --tmpfs /home/ard:uid=65532,gid=65532,exec --tmpfs /var/tmp:uid=65532,gid=65532,exec \\\n     -v \"$ROOT:/repo:ro\" -w /repo --entrypoint /repo/.ard/scanners/cve/scan \"$tag\" >\"$scan_out\" 2>\"$work/scan.err\"; then\n  if [ ! -s \"$scan_out\" ] || jq -e -s 'all(.[]; type == \"object\" and has(\"fingerprint\"))' \"$scan_out\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    pass \"cve scan runs in the image as a host subprocess\"\n  else\n    fail \"cve scan runs in the image as a host subprocess\" \"stdout is not finding JSON lines: $(head -c 300 \"$scan_out\")\"\n  fi\nelse\n  fail \"cve scan runs in the image as a host subprocess\" \"exit $?: $(tail -5 \"$work/scan.err\")\"\nfi\n\n# The same run on a fixture with a known-vulnerable dependency must report it: empty output cannot pass.\nfixture=\"$ROOT/deploy/testdata/cve-fixture\"\nfx_out=\"$work/fixture.out\"\nif run --tmpfs /home/ard:uid=65532,gid=65532,exec --tmpfs /var/tmp:uid=65532,gid=65532,exec \\\n     -v \"$fixture:/repo:ro\" -v \"$ROOT/.ard/scanners/cve:/role:ro\" -w /repo --entrypoint /role/scan \"$tag\" \\\n     >\"$fx_out\" 2>\"$work/fixture.err\" \\\n   && jq -e -s 'length >= 1 and all(.[]; type == \"object\" and (.fingerprint | startswith(\"osv:\")))\n                and any(.[]; .fingerprint | test(\":golang.org/x/text$\"))' \"$fx_out\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  pass \"cve scan in the image reports the fixture's known vulnerability\"\nelse\n  fail \"cve scan in the image reports the fixture's known vulnerability\" \"$(head -c 300 \"$fx_out\") $(tail -3 \"$work/fixture.err\")\"\n107:    worker-engineer.md         # worker: what changed, test results, open issues\n181:**LLM scanners (`prompt.txt`) file under extra rules.** `autoclose: true` on one is a config error: an LLM scanner never closes an issue for absence. `every` defaults to at least 24h and a value below 24h is an error; `max_budget_usd`, when set, must be above 0. An LLM fingerprint is `rule:path` or `rule:path:symbol` (rule `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}`, a clean relative path without `..` that is also listed in the finding's `files` and exists, symbol `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_./-]{0,127}`); a bad fingerprint is reported by finding index only, never by content. Before grouping, a finding whose title is a near-duplicate of an open issue's title or checklist line, or of a more severe finding in the same run, is skipped without a ledger row, so it is offered again next run. Titles are lowercased, reduced to `[a-z0-9]` tokens, minus one-character tokens and a short stop list; they match when the token sets are equal, or when both have at least three tokens and their Jaccard similarity is at least 0.8. An issue filed by a scanner whose route is not `pending` ends with a footer explaining that a human adds `ard:pending` to send it to triage or closes it as not planned to dismiss it. The stale closer also considers an open issue whose only `ard:` label is its scanner label (route `none`). The engine that runs `prompt.txt` scanners is not wired in yet.\n211:| lts | endoflife.date + manifests: go.mod, package.json, Dockerfile `FROM`, Helm charts | 7 d | 1 per component | yes |\n229:**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; only HEAD is scanned, and a history scanner is a follow-up (#224). The forbidden flags (`--since-commit`, `--max-depth`, `--detector-timeout`, `--archive-timeout`, `--filter-unverified`, `--filter-entropy`, `--results`) are never passed, because a partial or load-dependent result would autoclose live findings. Caveats: (1) a secret gone from HEAD is not revoked; autoclose closes the issue as completed, so the issue body tells the human to rotate first. (2) The fingerprint hash is unsalted, an offline oracle for a low-entropy value that is already in the repo at HEAD; accepted. (3) Issues on a public repo show `file:line`. (4) At `max_open` the scanner is skipped, so new leaks go unseen until a human resolves one; scanner failure streaks are posted on the status issue (#221). (5) Any future change that commits scanner logs must exclude this scanner. (6) On a public repo with GitHub secret scanning off, this scanner is the only detection channel, and it cannot stop a push. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile (or a host install) and is not in the published `ard` image or the root `Dockerfile`. Host and Docker integration tests (`go test -tags integration -run Trufflehog ./internal/roles/`, with `ARD_TRUFFLEHOG_BIN` at the Dockerfile's pinned version) prove the role against a real binary and image.\n280:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n318:A worker implements `plan.md` on its own branch and ends with a draft PR, test results, and a summary on the issue.\n384:- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, and the linter config a PR could weaken, `**/.golangci.yml`, `**/.golangci.yaml`, `**/.golangci.toml` and `**/.golangci.json` (17 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix; any other entry is a doublestar glob. Matching is case-insensitive on the repo-relative path (macOS file systems are, so `Claude.md` and `.Claude/x` are protected). A prefix matches on a directory boundary (`.github/` matches `.github` and `.github/x`, never `.githubx`). A glob follows doublestar: `*` stays within one path segment and `**` crosses them, so a bare `CODEOWNERS` would match only the root file. Quote globs that start with `*` in YAML. The merger checks both names of a renamed file; a malformed path counts as protected. A PR that touches one gets `ard:needs-approval` and merges on the next poll once a person with write access approves the current head with a GitHub review (below).\n422:intake: { mode: label }     # mode: all | label; label (label mode only) defaults to ard:pending, must start with ard: and must not be one of ard's own labels\n423:merge:                       # defaults written by `ard init`; the list is the only source (no floor in code)\n450:**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`,